    "parsing_res_list": [
        {
            "block_label": "header",
            "block_content": "100\nHEAT CONDUCTION EQUATION",
            "block_bbox": [
                79,
                10,
                451,
                54
            ],
            "block_id": 0,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "inline_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ k=1.2W/m\\cdot K $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                1212,
                61,
                1420,
                95
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 1
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Properties The thermal conductivity is given to be  $ k = 1.2 \\, Wh/m \\cdot K $ .\n\nAnalysis Taking the direction normal to the surface of the wall as the x-direction with its origin on the inner surface, the differential equation for this problem can be expressed as",
            "block_bbox": [
                558,
                78,
                1573,
                226
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ \\frac{d^{2}T}{dx^{2}}=0 $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                990,
                231,
                1109,
                305
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "with boundary conditions",
            "block_bbox": [
                551,
                327,
                881,
                363
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ \\begin{aligned}T(0)&=T_{1}=300K\\\\-k\\frac{dT(L)}{dx}&=\\varepsilon\\sigma[T(L)^{4}-T_{space}^{4}]-\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}\\end{aligned} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                788,
                377,
                1312,
                494
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "where  $ T_{space} = 0 $ . The general solution of the differential equation is again obtained by two successive integrations to be",
            "block_bbox": [
                538,
                518,
                1564,
                590
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(x)=C_{1}x+C_{2} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                916,
                610,
                1140,
                646
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7
        },
        {
            "block_label": "formula_number",
            "block_content": "(a)",
            "block_bbox": [
                1522,
                610,
                1558,
                642
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "where  $ C_{1} $  and  $ C_{2} $  are arbitrary constants. Applying the first boundary condition yields",
            "block_bbox": [
                529,
                664,
                1560,
                731
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 8
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(0)=C_{1}\\times0+C_{2}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad C_{2}=T_{1} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                809,
                756,
                1274,
                795
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 9
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Noting that  $ dT/dx = C_{1} $  and  $ T(L) = C_{1}L + C_{2} = C_{1}L + T_{1} $ , the application of the second boundary conditions gives",
            "block_bbox": [
                525,
                818,
                1556,
                889
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 10
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ -k\\frac{dT(L)}{dx}=\\varepsilon\\sigma T(L)^{4}-\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}\\ \\rightarrow\\ -kC_{1}=\\varepsilon\\sigma(C_{1}L+T_{1})^{4}-\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                584,
                903,
                1493,
                978
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 11
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Although  $ C_{1} $  is the only unknown in this equation, we cannot get an explicit expression for it because the equation is nonlinear, and thus we cannot get a closed-form expression for the temperature distribution. This should explain why we do our best to avoid nonlinearities in the analysis, such as those associated with radiation.",
            "block_bbox": [
                509,
                1000,
                1561,
                1174
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 12
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Let us back up a little and denote the outer surface temperature by  $  T(L) = T_{L}  $  instead of  $  T(L) = C_{1}L + T_{1}  $ . The application of the second boundary condition in this case gives",
            "block_bbox": [
                502,
                1176,
                1564,
                1283
            ],
            "block_id": 14,
            "block_order": 13
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ -k\\frac{dT(L)}{dx}=\\varepsilon\\sigma T(L)^{4}-\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad-kC_{1}=\\varepsilon\\sigma T_{L}^{4}-\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                624,
                1305,
                1444,
                1380
            ],
            "block_id": 15,
            "block_order": 14
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Solving for  $ C_{1} $  gives",
            "block_bbox": [
                497,
                1406,
                773,
                1444
            ],
            "block_id": 16,
            "block_order": 15
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ C_{1}=\\frac{\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}-\\varepsilon\\sigma T_{L}^{4}}{k} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                871,
                1466,
                1156,
                1547
            ],
            "block_id": 17,
            "block_order": 16
        },
        {
            "block_label": "formula_number",
            "block_content": "(b)",
            "block_bbox": [
                1524,
                1496,
                1559,
                1529
            ],
            "block_id": 18,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Now substituting  $ C_{1} $  and  $ C_{2} $  into the general solution (a), we obtain",
            "block_bbox": [
                495,
                1565,
                1414,
                1609
            ],
            "block_id": 19,
            "block_order": 17
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(x)=\\frac{\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}-\\varepsilon\\sigma T_{L}^{4}}{k}x+T_{1} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                808,
                1629,
                1215,
                1710
            ],
            "block_id": 20,
            "block_order": 18
        },
        {
            "block_label": "formula_number",
            "block_content": "(c)",
            "block_bbox": [
                1531,
                1667,
                1568,
                1699
            ],
            "block_id": 21,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "which is the solution for the variation of the temperature in the wall in terms of the unknown outer surface temperature  $ T_{L} $ . At x = L it becomes",
            "block_bbox": [
                492,
                1729,
                1572,
                1817
            ],
            "block_id": 22,
            "block_order": 19
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T_{L}=\\frac{\\alpha\\dot{q}_{solar}-\\varepsilon\\sigma T_{L}^{4}}{k}L+T_{1} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                817,
                1833,
                1204,
                1913
            ],
            "block_id": 23,
            "block_order": 20
        },
        {
            "block_label": "formula_number",
            "block_content": "(d)",
            "block_bbox": [
                1518,
                1877,
                1558,
                1909
            ],
            "block_id": 24,
            "block_order": null
        }
    ],
